    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Director%2C-Helsinki-Institute-of-Physics-%28HIP%29/1371284057/?feedId=350602&utm_source=CareerSite_UniversityOfHelsinki": {
      "title": "Director, Helsinki Institute of Physics (HIP)",
      "description": "Director, Helsinki Institute of Physics (HIP) Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Director, Helsinki Institute of Physics (HIP) Helsinki Institute of Physics invites applications to the post of DIRECTOR to a fixed term position for 5 years starting 1.2.2027 or as agreed. The director is employed by the University of Helsinki and is appointed by the Rector of the University of Helsinki on the proposal of the Board of the Research Institute. The role of the director is to manage, develop and supervise the operations and finances of the research institute. The director has an overall responsibility for the department's personnel, finances and assets. The director is responsible for the general operation of the department and decides on internal matters in line with the HIP regulations. The director prepares the department's target program and budget and is responsible for the preparation and presentation of the matters to the board, as well as for the implementation of the board's decisions. The director is required to have a doctoral degree, familiarity with the field, and proven leadership skills. The applicant is expected to have research merits and international experience, as well as the ability to collaborate. The task requires a good command of English. The candidates are invited to write a short plan (1-2 page) on the development and reformation of the HIP activities in coming years. The application and its attachments are submitted via the electronic system of the University of Helsinki (link Apply now). Application period closes 5 October 2026. More information: Chair of the HIP Board, vice rector Kai Nordlund ( kai.nordlund@helsinki.fi , puh. +358 2941 50007) and vice director, research coordinator Antti Väihkönen (antti.vaihkonen@helsinki.fi, puh. +358 2941 50557). If you have any questions regarding the application system, please contact recruitment@helsinki.fi. The Helsinki Institute of Physics (HIP, www.hip.fi )  is a physics research institute that is operated jointly by the University of Helsinki, the University of Jyväskylä, Aalto University,  Lappeenranta–Lahti University of Technology (LUT), and Tampere University. In addition, the Finnish Radiation and Nuclear Safety Authority (STUK) is a fixed-term interim member. The mandate of the institute is to carry out and facilitate research in basic and applied physics, technology development at accelerator laboratories as well as participate in the researcher education in the field. The institute is responsible for the Finnish research collaboration with CERN. Also, the institute coordinates the Finnish contribution to the FAIR laboratory (Facility for Antiproton and Ion Research) and participates in international research collaboration. The institute also carries out collaboration projects with universities in the field of particle physics. A diverse and equitable study and work culture is important to us. That is why we do our best to promote an inclusive university community. We encourage all qualified applicants from diverse backgrounds to apply for our positions. Click this link to read about accessibility and inclusivity at our University. JOIN US TO BUILD A BETTER WORLD – TOGETHER! #HelsinkiUniCareers Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 05.10.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Faculty of Science Sub-unit: Helsinki Institute of Physics (HIP) ID: 5198 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Join us: work for the future – work for the world. Any questions? See our recruitment system FAQ Apply now » Find similar jobs: Leadership specialist and support positions Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. CONTACT Data protection Contact us WHY US? Why University of Helsinki Why 